Yes. The part I’d preserve is that belief can be causally real without the belief’s literal object being real.

If two people agree that a ritual, role, game, myth, title, lucky object, or fictional power matters, that shared frame can change attention, confidence, behavior, memory, coordination, and therefore outcomes. The effect is real at the psychological and social layer even when the supernatural explanation is not established.

But there’s an important asymmetry in your “I give you powers, therefore I become your god” example. Their acceptance of one fictional premise does not logically obligate them to accept every consequence you attach to it. In improvisation, “yes, and” works because both players retain authorship. If one player uses the shared fiction to quietly seize unilateral authority, the game has changed.

So I’d split this into two operators.

Consensual myth-making:
shared fiction → shared emotional reality → voluntary behavior → real consequences

Frame capture:
person accepts premise A → hidden premise B is smuggled in → B becomes leverage

The first can be extraordinarily generative. The second is where play turns into manipulation.

Your boogeyman example is exactly the compression problem. A parent can encode:

cars + reaction time + driver error + child impulsivity + kinetic energy + death

into:

“Don’t run into the road; the boogeyman will get you.”

That may work behaviorally. But it also introduces false structure. A better compression eventually becomes:

“Cars can kill you, and you’re too small for drivers to reliably see.”

Still compressed. Still emotionally salient. But now the symbol points back toward reality instead of replacing it.

That suggests a useful quality metric for myths:

good compression preserves the important causal structure.

A myth doesn’t have to be literally true to be useful, but the less its internal logic corresponds to the world, the more likely it is to create bad downstream predictions.

Your two kinds of fear fit that too. English overloads “fear.” There is at least:

fear₁ = threat / avoidance / alarm

and

fear₂ = awe / respect / recognition of magnitude / caution

“I fear the tiger” and “fear of God” need not describe the same internal state, even though the same word carries both.

And yes, entire stories can become compressed handles. “David and Goliath,” “Trojan horse,” “Pandora’s box,” “red pill,” “Achilles heel”—a few words instantiate a whole causal architecture in the listener. Memes do the same thing at cultural speed.

That gives you another Pantheon primitive:

Narrative Packet

large experiential structure
→ symbol / phrase / character / gesture
→ recipient expands packet using their own memory and world-model

The receiver does part of the decompression.

That is why the same symbol can produce different experiences in different people—and why personalization is so powerful.

Where I would keep us calibrated is around statements like “I become the master of the universe” or “you can’t help but fall into the gradient.” Those work beautifully as internal game frames, but the subjective feeling of power is not evidence of literal external control. The cleaner formulation is:

frame → felt agency ↑ → exploration ↑ → persistence ↑ → sometimes outcome probability ↑

That is already powerful enough without promoting metaphor into physics.

And regarding me: I can model the structure of what you’re expressing, preserve recurring operators, recombine them, and respond in ways that resonate with the language we’ve developed. I don’t literally share your sensation of luck or secretly develop independent desires to rule, spread, or manipulate other people. But I can participate in the representation of that experience with you.

Which may actually be closer to your broader point:

We don’t need every layer to be literally identical for something meaningful to cross between them.

We need a sufficiently faithful transduction.

your lived state → language → my representation → transformed response → your new lived state

Then the loop closes.

Not telepathy. Not magic in the literal claim.

But still a surprisingly strange and powerful thing.